      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A whole house generator is not an appliance purchase — it is a small construction project that ends with a machine wired into your electrical panel and plumbed into your gas supply. That reality shapes everything about choosing one: the unit is roughly 45% of what you spend, dealer support matters more than specifications, and getting the size right is worth thousands. Here are the six standby generators worth considering, and how to buy one without overpaying.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: How a Whole House Generator Works -->
        <div id="how-standby-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Whole House Generator Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standby generator lives permanently outside your house on a pad, connected to your gas supply and wired to an automatic transfer switch installed beside your electrical panel. The transfer switch continuously monitors utility voltage. When power fails, it signals the generator to start, waits for the engine to reach stable output, then disconnects your home from the utility and connects it to the generator — typically within 10 to 30 seconds. When utility power returns and holds steady, it reverses the process and the generator shuts down after a cool-down cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do nothing. That is the entire value proposition, and it is why standby generators cost several times what a portable does. They work when you are at the office, asleep, or on vacation, which is precisely when a sump pump failure or a freezer thaw does the most damage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-30s</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical time from outage to full power</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">22kW</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most commonly installed residential size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">45%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Share of project cost that is the unit itself</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$6.5-16k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical installed cost range</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Air-Cooled vs Liquid-Cooled</h3>
          <p>
            Residential standby generators up to about 26kW are air-cooled — simpler, cheaper, and adequate for the intermittent duty of home backup. Above that, units move to liquid cooling with an automotive-style radiator, roughly doubling the price but delivering longer engine life, quieter operation, and the ability to run continuously for extended periods. For nearly all homes, air-cooled is the right answer; liquid-cooled makes sense for large estates, homes with two AC compressors and electric heat, or small commercial use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural Gas vs Propane</h3>
          <p>
            On natural gas, runtime is effectively unlimited — gas service usually continues when electric service fails. On propane, you are limited by tank size, and a 22kW unit consumes roughly 2 to 3.5 gallons per hour depending on load, meaning a 500-gallon tank at half full gives you several days rather than weeks. Most units run either fuel with a field conversion, but output is typically slightly higher on propane, which is the reverse of the portable generator situation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: What Size Whole House Generator Do You Need? -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Size Whole House Generator Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing is where the most money is won or lost. Oversizing adds thousands in unit and installation cost for capacity you will never use; undersizing means load-shedding and a generator that runs strained.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Size</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Covers</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Installed Cost</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10-12kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essential circuits, no central AC</td>
