                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14-18kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most of a smaller home, one AC zone</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8,000-11,500</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20-22kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typical whole home with central AC</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$9,000-13,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24-26kW</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large or all-electric homes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12,000-16,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30kW+ liquid-cooled</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Estates, two AC compressors, light commercial</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$18,000-35,000</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deciding Factor Is Almost Always Air Conditioning</h3>
          <p>
            Central AC is the single largest load in most homes and the reason people step up from 14kW to 22kW. A 3-ton compressor draws roughly 3,500-4,000 running watts with a startup surge well above that. If you can live without cooling during an outage, a much smaller generator covers everything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Load Management Modules Can Save You a Size</h3>
          <p>
            Smart management modules temporarily shed non-critical loads — typically an AC compressor or an electric water heater — when total demand spikes, then restore them. Two modules cost a few hundred dollars and can let an 18kW unit serve a house that would otherwise need 22kW, saving several thousand dollars. If your quote assumes the larger size, ask specifically whether load management closes the gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Panel vs Essential-Circuits Coverage</h3>
          <p>
            A whole-panel transfer switch backs up everything and requires a generator sized to the whole house. An essential-circuits switch backs up a selected subset — furnace, refrigerator, sump pump, well pump, some outlets and lights — and lets a much smaller generator do the job. The second option is dramatically cheaper and covers what genuinely matters for most households.
          </p>
        </div>

