        <!-- SECTION 4: Transfer Switches: The Part That Makes It Automatic -->
        <div id="transfer-switches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Transfer Switches: The Part That Makes It Automatic
          </h2>

          <p>
            The transfer switch is what separates a standby generator from an expensive portable. It is also a meaningful line item — $700 to $1,800 for the hardware — and the choice affects both cost and coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Panel (Service-Rated) Switches</h3>
          <p>
            Installed at the service entrance ahead of your main panel, these transfer the entire house to generator power. Everything works exactly as it does normally, which is the simplest experience, but the generator must be sized for the whole house. Typically 200 amp, and the most expensive option.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Essential-Circuit Switches</h3>
          <p>
            A sub-panel with a fixed number of circuits — commonly 8, 10, 12, or 16 — that you select during installation: furnace, refrigerator, sump pump, well pump, key lighting, and outlets. Much cheaper, and it lets a 10-14kW generator do the work of a 22kW unit. The compromise is that anything not on the list stays dark, and adding circuits later means an electrician visit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smart Switches With Load Management</h3>
          <p>
            The middle ground, and increasingly the default. A whole-panel switch combined with load management modules that shed the AC compressor or water heater when demand spikes. You get whole-house coverage with a smaller generator, at the cost of occasionally having the air conditioning pause for a few minutes while the dryer runs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Confirm the Switch Is in Your Quote</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Some quotes list the generator price prominently and treat the transfer switch as an allowance or an add-on. Since it can be a $1,800 item plus electrical labor to install, verify explicitly which switch model is included, how many circuits it covers, and whether load management modules are part of the price.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Installation: What Really Happens -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installation: What Really Happens
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation involves three trades and a permit process, and it accounts for roughly 55% of what you spend. Knowing the sequence helps you evaluate quotes and spot the surprises before they become change orders.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Site Assessment and Gas Capacity Check</h3>
          <p>
            The unit needs a level pad with specified clearances from windows, doors, vents, and property lines — commonly 18 inches from the house and five feet from openings, though local code governs. The bigger issue is gas capacity: a 22kW generator consumes roughly 300 cubic feet per hour, and many homes have a meter and service line sized only for a furnace, water heater, and range. When the utility has to upsize, it adds $1,500-3,000 and weeks of scheduling. Make this verification a condition before signing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pad</h3>
          <p>
            A poured concrete pad or a composite pad, both acceptable in most jurisdictions. Composite pads cost less and install faster; poured concrete is preferred in areas with significant frost heave. Either way it must be level, above local flood elevation, and sized to the unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electrical and Gas Work</h3>
          <p>
            An electrician installs the transfer switch beside your panel, runs conduit and conductors to the generator, and lands the control wiring. A gas fitter runs and sizes the supply line, installs a sediment trap and shutoff, and pressure-tests the run. Distance drives cost on both — every foot between the generator, the meter, and the panel adds material and labor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits, Inspection, and Startup</h3>
          <p>
            Electrical and gas permits are usually separate, with an inspection for each. After sign-off, the dealer performs a commissioning startup: verifying gas pressure under load, checking transfer operation with a simulated outage, and configuring the exercise schedule. Never accept an installation that skips a full load-transfer test in your presence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Fuel, Maintenance, and Ownership Cost -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel, Maintenance, and Ownership Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Standby generators are cheap to own and expensive to install. Here is the ongoing picture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel</h3>
          <p>
            A 22kW unit on natural gas costs roughly $3-5 per hour at moderate load, or $70-120 for a full day of outage at 2026 residential rates. On propane the same unit runs closer to $6-9 per hour. The weekly exercise cycle — typically 5 to 12 minutes — costs a few dollars a month.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance</h3>
          <p>
            An annual service, or every 100-200 run hours: oil and filter, air filter, spark plugs, valve clearance check, and battery test. Dealer maintenance contracts run $200-450 per year. Doing it yourself with the manufacturer's maintenance kit costs $60-150 in parts and roughly an hour, and does not void residential warranties provided you keep records.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Battery Is the Weak Link</h3>
          <p>
            The single most common cause of a standby generator failing to start is a dead starting battery — exactly like a car. Budget $80-150 every three to four years and treat any battery warning on the controller or app as urgent rather than routine. Many generators that "failed during the storm" simply had a four-year-old battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ten-Year Total</h3>
          <p>
            A typical 22kW installation: roughly $11,000 installed, $3,000 in maintenance, two batteries, and fuel for perhaps 60 hours of outages across the decade. That lands around $15,000 over ten years, or about $125 a month for guaranteed power. Whether that is worth it depends almost entirely on how often your area actually loses power and what an outage costs you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: How to Buy Without Overpaying -->
        <div id="buying-advice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Buy Without Overpaying
          </h2>

          <p>
            The spread between quotes for identical work is routinely $3,000-4,000, which makes the shopping process worth real effort.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get Three Itemized Quotes</h3>
          <p>
            Require each quote to break out the generator, transfer switch, electrical labor, gas work, pad, permits, and startup separately. A single bundled number makes comparison impossible and hides where an installer is padding. Any reputable dealer will itemize without argument.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Dealer, Not Just the Brand</h3>
          <p>
            You will be dealing with this company for annual service and warranty work for the next fifteen years. Ask how long they have been installing, how many units they service, what their emergency response looks like during a regional outage, and whether they stock parts. A great generator with an unresponsive installer is a worse outcome than a good generator with an excellent one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shop in the Off-Season</h3>
          <p>
            Demand and pricing spike after every major storm. Late winter and early spring consistently bring shorter lead times and more willingness to discount, since dealers are filling schedules rather than triaging a backlog.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Runs Short</h3>
          <p>
            Every foot between the generator, the gas meter, and the electrical panel adds conduit, wire, pipe, trenching, and labor. Siting the unit close to both — while respecting clearance requirements — is the largest cost you actually control, often worth $1,000-2,500.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Warranty Terms</h3>
          <p>
            Standard coverage is typically five years, with extended options available. Check whether it covers parts and labor or parts only, whether it requires dealer-performed maintenance to stay valid, and whether it transfers to a subsequent homeowner. That last point has real resale value.
          </p>
        </div>

