          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best whole house generator in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Generac Guardian 22kW is our overall pick — it is the size most homes need, it covers central air conditioning without load shedding, and it has by far the largest service network. For the best build quality, the Kohler 20RCA runs quieter and handles motor starting better, provided you have a good local Kohler dealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size whole house generator do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical 2,000-3,000 square foot home with central air conditioning, 20-22kW. For a smaller home or one where you can live without AC during an outage, 14kW paired with load management modules covers most needs for thousands less. Large or all-electric homes with two AC compressors need 26kW or a liquid-cooled unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a whole house generator cost installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 20-22kW unit typically runs $9,000-13,000 installed, of which the generator itself is roughly 45%. Smaller 10-14kW units run $6,500-9,000. The balance covers the automatic transfer switch, electrical labor, gas line work, pad, and permits. Gas meter upgrades, when required, add $1,500-3,000.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a whole house generator run continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On natural gas, effectively indefinitely, since gas service typically continues when electricity fails — though manufacturers recommend a maintenance check after roughly 100-200 run hours. On propane, runtime is limited by tank size: a 22kW unit consumes roughly 2 to 3.5 gallons per hour, so a 500-gallon tank at half capacity gives several days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit to install a standby generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in essentially all jurisdictions — usually separate electrical and gas permits, each with its own inspection. Permit fees typically run $150-800 total. A dealer who suggests skipping permits is proposing something that will complicate your home sale and may void insurance coverage on any resulting damage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a whole house generator need maintenance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annually, or every 100-200 run hours, whichever comes first: oil and filter, air filter, spark plugs, and a battery check. Dealer contracts run $200-450 per year, or you can do it with a manufacturer maintenance kit for $60-150 in parts. The starting battery needs replacement every three to four years and is the most common cause of no-start failures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a whole house generator add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In outage-prone regions, industry estimates commonly cite 3-5% of home value, though appraisers treat it inconsistently and it rarely returns the full installed cost. It is more reliably an advantage in how quickly a home sells than a dollar-for-dollar return. A transferable warranty strengthens the case.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Costly Mistakes When Buying a Whole House Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every expensive error in this category happens before the generator is ever delivered.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Verifying Gas Capacity Before Signing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 22kW generator consumes roughly 300 cubic feet of gas per hour, and many older homes have a meter sized for a furnace, water heater, and range. When the utility must upsize the meter or service line, it adds thousands and weeks of delay. Make gas capacity verification a written condition of the contract.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 22kW When 14kW Plus Load Management Would Do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The larger unit costs more, needs more gas — often triggering a meter upgrade — and takes more space. Two load management modules cost a few hundred dollars and can let a 14kW unit cover a house that would otherwise need 22kW. Ask for both configurations to be quoted before defaulting to the bigger machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Brand and Ignoring the Dealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You will need this company for annual service and warranty work for fifteen years, and for emergency response during a regional outage when every dealer is swamped. A great generator installed by an unresponsive company is worse than a good generator with an excellent local dealer behind it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Accepting a Bundled Single-Number Quote</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without separate lines for generator, transfer switch, electrical labor, gas work, pad, permits, and startup, you cannot compare bids or spot padding. Spreads of $3,000-4,000 on identical installations are routine, and itemization is the only way to see them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Battery Until It Fails</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
